      <h1>The Biggest Polluter: Amazon's Pecos County Gas Plant Could Out-Emit Every Power Plant in America</h1>

      <h2>📘 Introduction</h2>
      <p>Amazon just got permission to build what could become the single dirtiest power plant in the United States — and it isn't a coal utility or a fossil fuel major doing the building. It's an online retailer, powering its next wave of AI data centers. The Texas Commission on Environmental Quality has issued an air permit for a 7.65-gigawatt, 35-turbine natural gas plant in Pecos County authorized to release up to 33 million tons of carbon dioxide a year. That would make it the largest single point source of CO2 emissions from any facility in the country — more than double the roughly 16 million tons a year put out by Alabama's coal-fired James H. Miller Jr. plant, currently the record-holder.</p>
      <p>The plant, reported to be known internally as the "GW Ranch" project and developed by Pacifico Energy, exists for one purpose: keeping Amazon's AI data centers running around the clock, entirely off the public grid.</p>

      <h2>🏗️ What Actually Got Permitted</h2>
      <p>The permit covers 35 gas turbines totaling 7.65 gigawatts, paired with 1.8 gigawatts of battery storage and 750 megawatts of solar. It's built as a private, "behind-the-meter" campus — meaning it generates and consumes its own power without ever connecting to ERCOT's grid. That detail matters more than it sounds: it's the same reason this project can move on its own timeline, insulated from the interconnection queue and the state-level scrutiny that's been slowing down grid-connected projects all year.</p>
      <p>The site sits in the same stretch of West Texas where Microsoft is already building a 2-gigawatt off-grid data center campus of its own, announced back in June. Satellite imagery showed land clearing underway at the Amazon site by late July, and Amazon filed three separate Texas data center construction permits in early August — the same week our own briefing flagged the company locking down roughly 8,000 acres in Pecos County.</p>

      <h2>💨 The Emissions Math</h2>
      <p>Thirty-three million tons of CO2 a year is the ceiling TCEQ authorized, not a guarantee of what the plant will actually emit once running — but it's the number regulators used to size the permit, and it's what makes this a national story rather than a regional one. For comparison, the current largest emitting facility in the U.S. power sector, the James H. Miller Jr. coal plant in Alabama, emits roughly 16 million tons annually. A single Texas AI campus, on paper, could nearly double that.</p>
      <p>It's also arriving at the same time Amazon's own corporate emissions are climbing. The company's reported carbon footprint rose roughly 16% in the most recent year on record, driven largely by the buildout of AI infrastructure — the exact category of facility this plant was built to serve.</p>

      <h2>🌎 The Climate Pledge Problem</h2>
      <p>Amazon co-founded the Climate Pledge in 2019, committing to eliminate its carbon emissions by 2040. Every year since, the company's actual emissions have gone up, not down. Asked about the gap between that pledge and a permit for the country's largest potential single source of CO2, Amazon spokeswoman Margaret Callahan told reporters: "The world looks different now than when we co-founded the climate pledge," while maintaining that "our commitment hasn't changed." Amazon has separately said the plant "won't raise electricity costs for Texas families" — true in the narrow sense that a behind-the-meter plant doesn't touch ERCOT pricing, but emissions don't stop at a property line, and the air quality impact doesn't check whose meter the power ran through.</p>

      <h2>🕳️ The Regulatory Blind Spot</h2>
      <p>This is the part that should matter most to Texans watching the broader data center fight: Governor Abbott's freeze on new ERCOT grid connections — the one meant to force an audit of tax breaks, water use, and ownership on the state's 1,800-plus queued projects — doesn't touch this plant at all, because it was never planning to connect to ERCOT in the first place. TCEQ's air permit process evaluates air quality impact. It does not weigh in on siting, land use, water draw, or grid reliability, and its public comment window is far narrower than the scrutiny an equivalent public utility plant would face before breaking ground.</p>
      <p>It's the same loophole we've flagged before: a Bexar County gas-and-diesel plant accused of splitting itself into smaller permits to dodge public review, and a Dallas-area company pitching a floating AI data center specifically because it skips both the ERCOT queue and a new water permit at once. Off-grid, behind-the-meter power has become the industry's preferred way around exactly the kind of scrutiny that's slowing down grid-connected projects statewide — and a 33-million-ton-a-year gas plant is the starkest example yet of how much can clear that path with a single air permit.</p>

      <h2>🧭 What Texans Should Be Watching</h2>
      <p>Whether TCEQ or federal regulators revisit a permit of this size as national attention grows. What air quality actually looks like for Pecos County communities once 35 turbines are running, in a rural region with little existing industrial monitoring. And whether this becomes the template — gas-fired, off-grid, unconstrained by the ERCOT freeze — for the rest of Amazon's Texas buildout, or for competitors racing to avoid the same interconnection bottleneck.</p>

      <h2>🧾 Conclusion</h2>
      <p>Amazon's pitch is that this is private power for a private campus, not a public burden. But a permit authorizing 33 million tons of CO2 a year makes this the single largest point source of greenhouse gases in the country, cleared through a process built to evaluate air quality — not to weigh whether a company should be allowed to build the nation's biggest polluter in order to train AI models faster than the public grid can keep up.</p>
